💡 **SUGGESTION: Pin the measured latency budget next to `_waveOneSleepBase`**

`_waveOneSleepBase = 20` encodes a ~2× safety factor over the measured worst-case capture latency ("~10 s on fa-m5-class runners"), but that budget exists only in prose. The next person who retunes these sleeps (or the settle stagger) has to re-derive the arithmetic from git history.

Two observations supporting the current design — no change required for merge:

1. The structural protection is actually the `waitForScreen(_olderRow)` gate below: the frozen `· older` row only ever prints `5 running · 0 done` while all five jobs are still live, so if runner latency ever exceeds the base the test fails as a 60 s `TimeoutException` with a screen dump (diagnosable) rather than a false pass or the old empty-capture `[]` assertion. That makes the empirical margin acceptable.
2. If you do touch this, consider making the budget explicit, e.g.:

```dart
/// Measured worst-case capture latency (boot + 3 scripted turns + PTY
/// polling) on fa-m5-class runners; keep `_waveOneSleepBase` ≈ 2× this.
const int _captureLatencyBudgetS = 10;
const int _waveOneSleepBase = 2 * _captureLatencyBudgetS;
```

Also worth noting for the record: raising the base from 5→20 has a beneficial side effect on the second test in this file (restart-lost REG) — `hardKill()` now always precedes the first settle, so the asserted `5 background tasks lost` count can no longer race an early settle on a slow runner.

